    "chars": 1263,
    "text": " (a) The function `merge_intervals` takes a list of intervals and merges any overlapping intervals into a single interval. It returns the merged intervals in sorted order.\n\n(b) **Time Complexity**: O(n log n), due to sorting the intervals at the beginning. The subsequent loop runs in O(n).\n**Space Complexity**: O(n), for storing the result and potentially the sorted list if not done in place.\n\n(c) A correctness bug is that the function does not handle cases where `intervals` is an empty list correctly. It should return an empty list instead of attempting to access `sorted_intervals[0]`, which would raise an IndexError. The fix would be to check for an empty list before proceeding with any operations:\n```python\nif not intervals:\n    return []\n```\n\n(d) A performance improvement can be made by avoiding the conversion between a list and a tuple in each iteration of the loop. This is done on line 7 where `current` is converted to a tuple and then appended to `result`. Instead, appending directly to a list (which remains mutable throughout the function) would avoid this overhead:\n```python\nif start <= current[1]:\n    current[1] = max(current[1], end)\nelse:\n    result.append(current)  # Append as a list instead of tuple\n    current = [start, end]\n```",
